{"type": "message", "message": "**Minimalist means less visual clutter—not less comfort.** In a small flat, it’s about choosing a complete, useful set of furniture that works together, rather than leaving rooms empty.\n\n- **Simple shapes:** clean-lined furniture, with fewer competing details.\n- **A restrained palette:** your warm off-white walls could pair with light wood and one accent colour.\n- **Practical storage:** closed cupboards keep everyday things accessible but out of sight.\n- **Clear routes:** arrange furniture so moving between doors, seating and windows feels straightforward.\n- **Warmth through texture:** a rug, soft upholstery and a few personal objects keep it welcoming.\n\nFor your combined living-and-dining room, I’d favour two clear zones—a comfortable seating group and a compact dining area—rather than small pieces scattered throughout. That’s a design direction, not a change to your scene.", "conversationId": "2708330ff78242dca8c0ec93ee2ab86a"}
